{"name":"Malibu Blue","hex":"#00a9da","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"malibu","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of lightweight longboard known for its stability."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of Malibu bay breeze"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/malibu"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Malibu (Hole song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Malibu\" is a song by American alternative rock band Hole. It is the fourth track and second single from the band's third studio album, Celebrity Skin, and was released in December 1998, on DGC Records. The lyrics were written by vocalist and rhythm guitarist Courtney Love, with music written by Love, lead guitarist Eric Erlandson and Billy Corgan of the Smashing Pumpkins, who contributed to four other songs on Celebrity Skin."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/57/Holemalibu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malibu_(Hole_song)","wikidata":"Q3325531"},{"title":"Malibu (rum)","blocks":[{"text":"Malibu is a coconut flavoured liqueur made with white rum, which has an alcohol content by volume of 21.0%. Since 2005 the Malibu brand has been owned by Pernod Ricard, which calls it a \"flavored rum\", where this designation is allowed by local laws."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c4/Malibu_%28rum%29_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malibu_(rum)","wikidata":"Q177092"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ed/Hole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg/250px-Hole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ed/Hole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ed/Hole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg/250px-Hole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHole_Malibu_alternate_cover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Mirny%2C_Malibu_Caribbean_Rum_with_Coconut%2C_Psychedelic%2C_Crimea.jpg/500px-Mirny%2C_Malibu_Caribbean_Rum_with_Coconut%2C_Psychedelic%2C_Crimea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Mirny%2C_Malibu_Caribbea